Init_Debris @0x424090: Setup_Sprite reads the destination block's ABR byte (+9)
BEFORE initializing it (FUN_00416764 @0x41694f: mov 0x9(%esi),%bl; shl $5; or ->
tpage blend bits).  Init_Debris passes two uninitialized stack blocks, so the
ORIGINAL's debris tpage ABR bits come from deterministic stack leftovers: at
entry (ret=0x445ebf, Init_Main), block A lies over the preceding
InitialiseParticleSystem frame; A+9 = byte 1 of the leftover pushed argument
s_SPARK_0046cdcc = 0xcd, B+9 = 0x00 (both measured in the reference via entry-
stack snapshot; A[6] then = 0x19a3, matching the 66 debris prim fields that had
diverged since cf3 -- our zero-initialized stack yielded 0x0683).  Effect chain
proven: wrong ABR -> debris vertex-z/OT-bucket shifts -> the cf55_1 87-px fb
split and the cf56+ cascade of the L9 attract.  Seed exactly the two bytes the
original reads, reproducing its deterministic frame state (same principle as
the FE-state replication); NOT a tuning constant.  Later attract cycles leave
different remnants -- documented as open in the task notes.

